WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.910
See your money clearly: needs, wants, income and leaks.

2
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:09.230
This is the video for Module 1, Understanding Money and Your Financial Life.

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:21.640
Financial literacy simply means understanding money well enough to make good choices with it. A market woman who knows what each bag of rice cost her is practising it every day.

4
00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:27.640
Almost every money choice falls into one of five decisions. Earn is how money comes in.

5
00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.970
Spend is what you pay for, and whether it is worth it.

6
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.160
Save is what you keep for later.

7
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.930
Borrow is when you use someone else's money, and at what cost.

8
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:48.600
And protect is keeping your money and family safe from shocks and fraud, like never sharing your BVN or OTP.

9
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:54.860
A need keeps you safe, healthy and earning. A want makes life nicer but can wait.

10
00:00:55.210 --> 00:01:04.470
Question one. What happens if I do not buy this today? If my family goes hungry, falls sick, loses shelter or I cannot work, it is a need.

11
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:17.930
Question two. Is there a cheaper version that meets the same need? Food is a need, but eating out every day is partly a want. The basic version is the need. The extra cost is the want.

12
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:27.790
Question three. Can it wait 30 days? Then write it on a wants list instead of buying it now. Many items drop off the list on their own.

13
00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:34.570
Wants are not bad. A small planned treat is better than a big unplanned one that leaves you short for rent.

14
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:45.760
For example, Aisha, a hairdresser in Kaduna, wrote down everything she spent in one week. Foodstuff, bus fare and hair products for clients were needs.

15
00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:58.080
But suya and soft drinks on three evenings cost about 7,500 naira. And a contribution for a friend's aso ebi was about 15,000 naira.

16
00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:05.750
Out of about 58,500 naira, about 22,500 was clearly wants.

17
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:16.580
She did not cut every want. She kept one suya evening and declined the aso ebi this time. That freed about 20,000 naira for her emergency savings.

18
00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:28.280
Plan with take-home money. For a business, that is profit, not total sales. When income changes month to month, plan with a careful average, not your best month.

19
00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:37.270
For example, Funke runs a small food canteen in Ibadan. Her profit over six months was about 560,000 naira.

20
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.170
That is an average of about 93,000 naira a month.

21
00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.930
Her lowest month was about 70,000.

22
00:02:47.180 --> 00:03:00.470
So she plans her home spending on 80,000 naira, between the lowest month and the average. In any month she makes more, the extra goes into a separate savings account. In a weak month, that buffer fills the gap.

23
00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:09.790
Most people know their big expenses but are surprised by the small ones. The only way to know is to track. Choose your tool.

24
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:18.650
Every time you spend, write the date, what you bought, and the amount. Do it immediately, or at the end of each day at the latest.

25
00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:25.730
Include every channel: cash, transfers, POS, airtime and data, and money given to others.

26
00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:39.520
Each week, group the spending into categories like food, transport and children. At month end, add each category and compare it with what you expected. The point is information, not guilt.

27
00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:54.900
For example, Blessing, a teacher in Port Harcourt, tracked one month. She spent about 800 naira a day on drinks and snacks at work. Over 22 working days, that is about 17,600 naira a month.

28
00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:09.950
Irregular costs like annual rent feel like emergencies, though we know they are coming. The fix is to divide by 12. Her rent of about 480,000 naira means about 40,000 a month.

29
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:21.840
Her school fees of 270,000 naira a year mean about 22,500 a month. Now those payments no longer feel like emergencies.

30
00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:32.030
When you read your bank or mobile money history, look for charges you do not recognise, forgotten subscriptions, or transfers you did not make.

31
00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:40.130
Report them to your bank immediately through its official app, branch or customer care line, never through a number someone sends you.

32
00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:51.020
Now draw your money map, sort a week of spending into needs and wants, build your income record, and start your 30-day tracker. Continue with the lessons below.
